#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565438 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565439 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/2f/2f36451290eda3db5221e1a5fdbc1647bbedafa97cd47e22d5523d457c4cb83e.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=r.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le r\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}z({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}-{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2})\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{r}^{3}z({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\pi /2}}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}{\\int }_{0}^{r}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{3}z\\operatorname{cos}2\\theta }}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565440 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ for $y\\le 0,$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/35/350b2dc98a09957c17f1ab629e21021c30fcccd9443068c722fe7565f04dcbfe.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+9={r}^{2}+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+1}}\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{r}^{2}+9}}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+1}}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565463 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565465 :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\nII. $c=1$ gives a circle\n\nIII. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}{{y}^{2}+1} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1 & =c({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(1-c){y}^{2} & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which is an ellipse. So, I is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curves are $x=\\pm 1,$ which consists of two lines. So, II is false.\n\n- If $c=2,$ the level curve is $2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=3,$ which is a hyperbola. So, III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565477 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565593 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(3 - 4 x)^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=3 - 4 x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6g^{5}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  &=-24(3 - 4 x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565600 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-1+2x)^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{6},\\,g=-1+2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1+2x)^{5} \\\\  &=12(-1+2x)^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565617 :question/problem "![](images/3f/3fa6928e03ea158677a7a95970bd4a5694fecf07e29898395945508ce55c2b69.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/2e/2e7f98a3a56071215be52dcf70f568d868b6314b5e4831828cb6a83a0cc51966.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+(2)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{29}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{29}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565618 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d36006c69a9e18e6b603641b4f0ce4f197c5be279b8c8cc4aa3921e3da98c41.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6b6d4c70f67251eacc9199fd18bb57f7b2ee6a84f24f33851e3f03e3a6796edd.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=4$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(4)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+16} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $2 \\sqrt{5}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565644 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,x) = ⟨  x,\\,  \\text{_____} ,\\, 5\\tan{t}⟩, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,x)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{y^2}{64} - \\frac{z^2}{25} = 1$ for $y \\ge 8.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{y^2}{64} - \\frac{z^2}{25} = 1$ for $y \\ge 8$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ y ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  8\\sec{t} ,\\, 5\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =\\frac{(8\\sec  t)^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(5\\tan  t)^{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\sec^{2} t}{64}-\\frac{25\\tan^{2} t}{25} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,8\\sec  t,\\,5\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $8\\sec{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565646 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y) = ⟨  \\text{_____} ,\\,  y,\\, 8\\tan{t}⟩, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\frac{z^2}{64} = 1$ for $x \\ge 7.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\frac{z^2}{64} = 1$ for $x \\ge 7$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ x ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  7\\sec{t} ,\\, 8\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{(7\\sec  t)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(8\\tan  t)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\sec^{2} t}{49}-\\frac{64\\tan^{2} t}{64} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,8\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $7\\sec{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565652 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨3\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5{x}^{2}-9{z}^{2}=45$ for $x\\ge 3.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-9{z}^{2}=45\\,⇒\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\ge 3$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,z⟩=⟨3\\sec  t,\\,\\sqrt{5}\\tan  t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =\\frac{(3\\sec  t)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5}\\tan  t)^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sec^{2} t}{9}-\\frac{5\\tan^{2} t}{5} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{5}\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{5}\\tan  t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565653 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,2\\sec  t,\\,z⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $y\\ge 2.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=4$ for $y\\ge 2$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y⟩=⟨2\\tan  t,\\,2\\sec  t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =(2\\sec  t)^{2}-(2\\tan  t)^{2} \\\\  & =4(\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\tan  t,\\,2\\sec  t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2\\tan  t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565656 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y) = ⟨  \\text{_____} ,\\,  y,\\, 4\\tan{t}⟩, \\qquad t\\in\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^2}{81} - \\frac{z^2}{16} = 1$ for $x \\ge 9.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{x^2}{81} - \\frac{z^2}{16} = 1$ for $x \\ge 9$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ x ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  9\\sec{t} ,\\, 4\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(9\\sec  t)^{2}}{81}-\\frac{(4\\tan  t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\sec^{2} t}{81}-\\frac{16\\tan^{2} t}{16} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,4\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $9\\sec{t}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566225 :question/problem "$\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})+8{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-8\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+8{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x-8\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566324 :question/problem "Let $c=⟨-6,\\sqrt{3},-3⟩$ and $d=⟨x,0,4⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $c$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $d$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\nc⊥d\\,⟹\\,c\\cdot d=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot x+\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot d & =0 \\\\ -6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566327 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ x \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot x+(-6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-24-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -24-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566335 :question/problem "Let $p=-3i+4j-k$ and $q=xi+4j.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot x+4\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =16-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 16-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =16 \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566342 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ x \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 6+(-6)\\cdot x+(-5)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -6x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566344 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)+x\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-4+4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ -4+4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567011 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-{z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567021 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567042 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567047 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567052 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (0+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567072 :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ for $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-x\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{4})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot x\\cdot 4{z}^{3} \\\\  & =-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567081 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =(3{x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567093 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differenciation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+{y}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567195 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>2\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2)\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$; $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567197 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne -4\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567216 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 1,\\,x-y\\ge 0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$; $U(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x-1},\\sqrt{x-y}⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567229 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>-1\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y)$ $=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+1)⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567231 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>-4\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $A(x,y)$ $=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+4)⟩$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567313 :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(-2)\\left((x+y)^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +((-2)\\left((y+z)^{2}\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +((-2)\\left((x+z)^{2}\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((-2)\\left((x+y)^{2}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}((-2)\\left((y+z)^{2}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}((-2)\\left((x+z)^{2}\\right))  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(2(x+y)+2(y+z)+2(x+z)\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4(x+y+z)\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,1,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\text{div}\\,F(-2,1,-1) \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4((-2)+(1)+(-1))\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(4\\cdot (-2)\\right)  \\\\ & =(-2)\\left(-8\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567319 :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\left({x}^{2}yz\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\left({x}^{3}y\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\left(z\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right))  \\\\ & =2\\left(2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\text{div}\\,F(-1,-1,2) \\\\ & =2\\left(2(-1)(-1)(2)\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +(-1)^{3}\\right. \\\\ & \\quad \\left. +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right)\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567333 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨- \\left(x - y\\right)^{3},\\,- 3 \\left(y + z\\right)^{3},\\,3 \\left(x + z\\right)^{3}⟩.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- \\left(x - y\\right)^{3}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 \\left(y + z\\right)^{3}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3 \\left(x + z\\right)^{3})  \\\\ & =(- 3 \\left(x - y\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(- 9 \\left(y + z\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(9 \\left(x + z\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =- 3 \\left(x - y\\right)^{2} + 9 \\left(x + z\\right)^{2} - 9 \\left(y + z\\right)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(2,1,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(2,1,-2) \\\\ & =(- 3 \\left((2) - (1)\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(- 9 \\left((1) + (-2)\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +(9 \\left((2) + (-2)\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =(-3)+(-9)+(0)  \\\\ & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567337 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨\\left(2 x + y\\right)^{2},\\,\\left(- 3 y + 3 z\\right)^{2},\\,\\left(3 x + 2 z\\right)^{2}⟩.$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(2 x + y\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(- 3 y + 3 z\\right)^{2}) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(3 x + 2 z\\right)^{2})  \\\\ & =(4 \\left(2 x + y\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(18 \\left(y - z\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(4 \\left(3 x + 2 z\\right))  \\\\ & =20 x + 22 y - 10 z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-1,-2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(-2,-1,-2) \\\\ & =(4 \\left(2 (-2) + (-1)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(18 \\left((-1) - (-2)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +(4 \\left(3 (-2) + 2 (-2)\\right))  \\\\ & =(-20)+(18)+(-40)  \\\\ & =-42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567343 :question/problem "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =2\\left(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\right)\\,i \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(xy{e}^{z}\\right))\\,j \\\\ & \\quad +(2\\left(z(x+1)\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& ∇\\cdot F \\\\ & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}  \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\left(x(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\left(xy{e}^{z}\\right)) \\\\ & \\quad +\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2\\left(z(x+1)\\right))  \\\\ & =2\\left((y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)+x{e}^{z}+(x+1)\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(y{e}^{z}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x{e}^{z}+x+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,1,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& (∇\\cdot F)(1,1,0) \\\\ & =2\\left(1\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{0}+1+1\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(1+0+1+1+1\\right)  \\\\ & =2\\left(4\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
